WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.236
- A while back, I made a promise.

00:00:03.236 --> 00:00:05.653
(soft music)

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.430
Someday, I'll make a video
about this whole messy debacle.

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:16.040
So today I intend to make
good on that promise.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:17.167
Here we go.

00:00:17.167 --> 00:00:19.750
(upbeat music)

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.760
This line is weird.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:33.750
It's not a border,

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.860
but I guess it kind of
is, it divides something.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.380
If it's Monday evening

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.020
and you fly from Hawaii to New Zealand,

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.730
you'll cross into Tuesday
evening and then back

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:46.912
into Monday evening and then
back into Tuesday evening.

00:00:46.912 --> 00:00:48.970
And because you've been
flying for a while,

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:51.460
now it's probably Wednesday morning.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:52.293
You just jumped

00:00:52.293 --> 00:00:54.570
from Monday to Wednesday
on a nine hour flight.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.960
Better yet, jump in your
boat on a Tuesday afternoon

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.930
and go a few hundred kilometers south.

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:01.600
And suddenly you're watching
the sunset on a Monday

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.960
because you drove south in your boat.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:06.153
Wait what?

00:01:06.153 --> 00:01:07.900
So I want to understand this line.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.130
So naturally the first thing I do is go

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:12.400
into After Effects and try to map it out.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.305
As I scoured the internet
for good data on this,

00:01:15.305 --> 00:01:17.140
I realized that everyone's
international dateline

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:18.630
is slightly different,

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:20.860
from Google Maps to the CIA fact book,

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.930
to the Pacific Island
Ocean Observing System,

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:26.390
which is an organization
that has the elegant acronym

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:27.560
of PacIOOS.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:34.230
Anyway, I spent the day
scraping all the data I could,

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:36.173
and trying to map this line.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.050
Looks like it's 195 miles
or around 300 kilometers.

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:45.530
That's a huge difference.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:48.480
There are entire islands
with people that Google

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.913
says are on one side and
PacIOOS say are on the other.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:54.900
Who's right here?

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.430
Before we settle that,

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.300
I want to dig into what this line even is

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.653
and why it looks the way it does.

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:08.600
Okay, so this whole crossing a line

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:11.990
and passing over a day
and going back in time

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:13.390
and all of that, is a little bit hard

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:15.250
for I know my brain to get around.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.377
And I imagine that a lot
of you came to this video,

00:02:17.377 --> 00:02:19.450
hoping that you will leave this video

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:22.840
with a firm grasp of what The
International Date Line is.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:26.070
So I'm going to dive in and
explain it the best I can.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.463
First, let's look at
the earth from top down.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:34.061
So we're looking at the earth
from above, like top down.

00:02:34.061 --> 00:02:35.860
Here's great Britain over here.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:36.693
And of course,

00:02:36.693 --> 00:02:38.950
we've got our beautiful sun
shining light onto our earth

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:41.310
which gives us day and night.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:45.940
Land that is rotating into
this light is seeing a sunrise,

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:48.230
land that is transitioning
out of this light

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:50.120
is seeing a sunset.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.130
And it's the relationship

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:54.230
to this light that we
measure our time with,

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:57.980
with noon being the point
where we are right smack dab

00:02:57.980 --> 00:02:59.470
in the middle of the sun's light.

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:02.300
So right now it's noon in Florida.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.640
Okay, you already know this,
but I'm setting it up here

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.800
because it helps the
Date Line makes sense.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:09.290
Let's go back to Britain,
which is upside down right now

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:11.680
because remember we're
looking down on the globe,

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.370
like we're looking down at the North Pole.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:15.470
Also, this circle is pretty sloppy.

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:17.930
Let's clean it up and
make it a nice square

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:19.390
so you can keep your eye on it.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:22.950
It's important because all
time is measured from here,

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:26.200
technically in a little
observatory called Greenwich.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:30.400
So let's say, here in London,
it is the middle of the day,

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:31.330
on a Monday.

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:33.410
Okay, so it's noon on a Monday,

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:35.746
the sun is shining right on London.

00:03:35.746 --> 00:03:38.980
So let's freeze time and
look what's happening

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:41.540
a few thousand kilometers east of here

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:43.980
in the middle of Russia, it's 6:00 PM.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:45.310
The sun is setting.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:49.120
It's still Monday, but the
sun is setting on Monday.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:53.480
At this same moment, frozen in
time, over here in East Asia,

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:55.890
we've got China and there's Japan.

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:58.230
It's still noon in London, remember that,

00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.510
but it's late over here.

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:00.750
The sun has gone down.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.480
It's 9:00 PM in Japan.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:03.410
It's still Monday.

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:04.320
It's just late.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.220
The sun went down hours ago.

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:08.090
These people are finishing Monday.

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:10.811
It's like the middle
of the night on Monday.

00:04:10.811 --> 00:04:12.450
It's noon in London.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.960
Let's zoom out and
realize that it's Monday,

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:16.270
all around the world.

00:04:16.270 --> 00:04:18.230
The more west you go from London

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:20.574
it is actually earlier on Monday.

00:04:20.574 --> 00:04:24.370
East of London, it's
late on a Monday night,

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:25.450
like we just saw.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:27.320
It's later and later the more east you go.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.347
This is The International Date Line.

00:04:29.347 --> 00:04:33.060
It divides whether or not
it's late on a Monday night

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:34.816
or early on a Monday morning.

00:04:34.816 --> 00:04:36.790
Now let's press play

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:40.620
on the globe and watch
it rotate into a new day.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.230
Remember that these
guys right here are like

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:45.680
at 11:00 PM, basically midnight.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.970
They're about to start Tuesday.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:50.104
I'll keep Monday as this orange circle

00:04:50.104 --> 00:04:52.970
and I'll use green for Tuesday.

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:53.883
Okay, press play.

00:04:55.435 --> 00:04:58.018
(upbeat music)

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:05.950
Okay stop, zoom in, here in
the middle of the Pacific.

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:08.900
It's almost 11:00 AM on Tuesday, right?

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:12.880
You're 12 hours ahead of
Greenwich Mean Time in London.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:16.660
Go right over this line and
you'll still be around 11:00 AM.

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:18.660
The sun is still shining in the same way,

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:23.000
but now you are 12 hours
behind Greenwich Mean Time,

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.400
but it's still Monday.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.120
You're 12 hours behind Greenwich.

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:28.220
It hasn't passed into a new day yet.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:30.207
And that's how you're
able to cross this line

00:05:30.207 --> 00:05:33.040
and go back a day or forward a day,

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:36.004
even though you only go
a few hundred kilometers.

00:05:36.004 --> 00:05:36.837
Okay.

00:05:36.837 --> 00:05:39.460
I'm sure you're tired of
looking at this top down globe.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:41.950
It's kind of disorienting
and not super intuitive.

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:43.760
So let's see what this
looks like on a map,

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.170
on a regular old map.

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:46.970
Here we are in the same exact scenario.

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:48.840
It's around 11:00 AM right here.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:52.310
And yet it's Tuesday here
and Monday over here.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:54.340
I'm going to call this side which is later

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:57.800
than London's time, it's east
of London, the Tuesday side.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:59.716
And I'm going to call this side,

00:05:59.716 --> 00:06:02.832
which is behind London or west
of London, the Monday side.

00:06:02.832 --> 00:06:05.370
I'll just use that, that's
my own little terminology.

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:06.460
I'm gonna start using that.

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:08.360
And no one else uses it like that,

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.830
but I'm just going to for
the rest of this video, okay.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:14.610
I'm interrupting all of this
conceptual non-intuitive stuff

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:15.740
for a little intermission.

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:18.395
I want you to just look
at this for a second.

00:06:18.395 --> 00:06:21.300
There are a bunch of beautiful
islands in the Pacific.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:23.350
This one's called Aitutaki.

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:24.350
That's really what it's called.

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:26.360
That was my best accent.

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:28.610
Just remember that The
International Date Line runs

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:31.010
through some of the most
beautiful ocean in the world,

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:33.360
and it's worth just
looking at it for a second.

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:38.590
Let's get going on this
question of why it's different

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:39.500
on every map.

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:42.050
Why does this line look so messed up,

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:43.560
and what's with this notch?

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:46.870
And why can't anyone agree
on what it should look like?

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:49.010
This is the part that
I knew nothing about.

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:50.623
And it's fascinating.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:56.370
Technically, according to
our little conceptual model

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:59.470
that we just did, the line
should just be like this,

00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:01.530
a straight line that's on
the exact opposite side

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:03.480
of the world as Greenwich.

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:04.730
Meaning if Greenwich is zero,

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:06.670
this would be the 180th Meridian.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:08.350
But as I've said many times before,

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:11.450
nature isn't super good at straight lines.

00:07:11.450 --> 00:07:13.979
So instead it looks like this.

00:07:13.979 --> 00:07:17.649
It starts out following the
180th Meridian as it should.

00:07:17.649 --> 00:07:20.721
Then it splits off right here to make sure

00:07:20.721 --> 00:07:23.900
that this Russian Island and
then the very eastern tip

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:25.720
of Russia don't get sliced in two,

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:27.950
to become part of different days.

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:29.080
As it accommodates Russia.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:32.810
It passes right here
between Russia and Alaska.

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:33.643
If you zoom in,

00:07:33.643 --> 00:07:36.560
you'll see that it passes right
through these two islands.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:38.440
These islands are just
a few kilometers away

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:39.273
from each other.

00:07:39.273 --> 00:07:41.480
The sun sets at the same
time on both these islands,

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:42.600
they're right next to each other.

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:44.230
The difference is the sun is setting

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:48.370
on a Tuesday here and on a Monday here.

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:51.700
After Russia, the line heads
back west to meet up with

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:55.210
the 180th, but it passes
it in order to keep all

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:59.960
of these islands on this side
because they belong to Alaska.

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:02.960
This notch goes all the way
over to accommodate this island

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.070
of Alaska, which is this
tiny outpost of 21 people.

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:09.980
It looks like the most western
part of the United States,

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:12.100
but really because it's on the other side

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:15.930
of the 180th Meridian,
everything left of this line

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:17.950
is actually in the Eastern hemisphere.

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:21.230
So this is technically
the eastern most part

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:23.190
of the US but whatever,
it doesn't really matter.

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:24.960
It's all, these are all fake lines anyway.

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:27.070
Okay, so we've zigzagged back and forth,

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:29.140
crossing the 180th Meridian a few times

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:31.600
to accommodate Russia
and the United States.

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:33.540
It finally gets back onto the 180th

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:36.940
and starts following it
south for a really long time.

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:39.760
Once it gets to the equator,
we begin the big game

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:41.630
of whose side are you on?

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:43.070
All these islands in the Pacific

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:45.520
and there are loads of them,

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:48.560
have to make a decision on whether

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:51.960
or not they want to be the
first people to start new days,

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:54.580
or the last people to end those days.

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:56.500
We've got this big notch here

00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:59.620
which basically exists
to accommodate Kiribati,

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:03.470
which is an island nation
of just like 112,000 people.

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:04.910
But this nation is spread

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:08.110
across a huge swath of ocean,
which spans an area greater

00:09:08.110 --> 00:09:09.640
than the continental United States.

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:11.010
It's huge.

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:13.450
It bounces around here to
make sure that Kiribati

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:16.110
is on the Eastern hemisphere time zone

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:18.200
with Australia and Asia,

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:20.400
and that other nations
like the Cook Islands

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:23.660
are on the western part,
like with the United States.

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:25.780
It dips down here between Samoa,

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:27.770
which is on the Tuesday
side and the islands

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:30.540
of American Samoa, which
are on the Monday side,

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:31.890
same with the United States.

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:34.130
From here, the line heads straight south,

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:36.206
but not along the 180th like it should,

00:09:36.206 --> 00:09:40.487
instead it follows
closer to like the 172nd.

00:09:40.487 --> 00:09:42.730
This has meant to allow a bunch

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:46.610
of these island nations to
stay in the Tuesday side

00:09:46.610 --> 00:09:48.180
so that they can be on the same date

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:51.270
as big regional countries
like Australia or New Zealand.

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:53.536
Then finally it meets up
with the 180th Meridian

00:09:53.536 --> 00:09:56.543
and it follows it all the
way down to Antarctica.

00:09:57.412 --> 00:09:58.245
Okay.

00:09:58.245 --> 00:10:00.850
So that is this line and why it exists

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:03.550
and why it zigzags and all
of its twists and turns.

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:04.860
So our two important questions here,

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:06.020
why does it look so weird,

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:08.630
and why is it different on every map?

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:10.900
The first one we can kind
of answer pretty easily

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:12.730
after seeing where the line goes,

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:15.410
which is this line is really flexible.

00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:16.370
It's not a real border.

00:10:16.370 --> 00:10:19.510
It doesn't denote any
land that somebody owns.

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:20.701
A regular border is defined

00:10:20.701 --> 00:10:23.650
through conflict or negotiation,

00:10:23.650 --> 00:10:26.150
and it denotes the
sovereignty of a country.

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:27.680
You own this and I own that.

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:29.520
That is what a normal border does.

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:30.530
This doesn't do that.

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:33.330
The Date Line is a decision

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:36.223
that every country can make for itself.

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:41.960
When they're in the
Pacific they can decide,

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:46.960
oh, I want to be on the same
date as Australia and Japan,

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:51.000
or I want to be on the same date as Hawaii

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:52.720
and the United States.

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:54.280
That is a unilateral decision

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:57.300
that no country has to
negotiate with others to make.

00:10:57.300 --> 00:10:59.810
The Pacific nation of Samoa has decided

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:02.080
it would be better off if it observed

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:05.400
a time zone closer to that
of Australia and New Zealand.

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:07.770
So it's declared, it
will switch to our side

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:09.157
of The International Date Line

00:11:09.157 --> 00:11:11.210
Before that big notch existed,

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:14.160
half of Kiribati was in the Tuesday side

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:17.170
and the other half was in the Monday side,

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:20.020
which just made it really
hard where it was like Friday

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:21.690
on this side, and if people wanted to get

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:23.730
in touch with people in their country,

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:25.620
it was already Saturday on their side

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:27.470
and so they were off for the weekend.

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:33.430
So they unilaterally decided
in 1995 to just change the line

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:36.750
and put the entire country
on the Tuesday side.

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:39.410
So every country decides
how they want to do it,

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:41.660
and because of that,
it's changed dramatically

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:44.940
over the years as countries
just make their own decision

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:47.280
on what date and times
that they want to be in.

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:49.870
So now to my second
question and the big one

00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:52.790
that I was very curious
about which is who's right?

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:55.140
After a day of fixating on this question

00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:57.796
and mapping and doing all
this stuff on the computer,

